Caching counts correctly has two traps: Spring's `@Cacheable` won't fire when `findOffset()` calls `count()` on the same object (self-invocation bypasses the proxy), and without per-key locking a popular first page triggers N identical `COUNT(*)` queries at the same instant (cache stampede). So instead of `@Cacheable`, the platform gets a small `CountCache` abstraction backed by Caffeine, which does per-key synchronous loading out of the box. Every repository gets it for free; services only decide the TTL.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Client
    participant R as OrderRepository
    participant CC as CountCache (Caffeine, per table)
    participant SR as StarRocks
    C->>R: findPage(page=0, includeTotal=true)
    R->>SR: SELECT ... LIMIT 101 OFFSET 0
    R->>CC: getOrLoad(key = count SQL + bound params)
    alt miss (first caller locks the key)
        CC->>SR: SELECT COUNT(*) ... WHERE filters
        SR-->>CC: 48213
    else hit / concurrent callers wait on the same key
        CC-->>R: 48213
    end
    R-->>C: { items, page:0, total:48213 }
```

## 1. Dependency (`platform-starrocks/pom.xml`)

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.github.ben-manes.caffeine</groupId>
    <artifactId>caffeine</artifactId>   <!-- version managed by the Boot BOM -->
</dependency>
```

## 2. Configuration

```yaml
starrocks:
  # ...jdbc-url, pool, etc. as before
  count-cache:
    enabled: true
    ttl: 5m          # global default; a TableDefinition may override
    max-entries: 10000
```

```java
// platform-starrocks/src/main/java/com/acme/platform/starrocks/StarRocksProperties.java
package com.acme.platform.starrocks;

import jakarta.validation.constraints.*;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.bind.DefaultValue;
import org.springframework.validation.annotation.Validated;

import java.time.Duration;

@Validated
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "starrocks")
public record StarRocksProperties(
        @NotBlank String jdbcUrl,
        @NotBlank String username,
        String password,
        @Min(1) int queryTimeoutSeconds,
        @Min(0) long slowQueryThresholdMs,
        Pool pool,
        @DefaultValue CountCache countCache) {          // @DefaultValue => instantiated even if the block is absent

    public record Pool(@Min(1) int maximumPoolSize, @Min(0) int minimumIdle,
                       long connectionTimeoutMs, long maxLifetimeMs, long keepaliveTimeMs) {}

    public record CountCache(@DefaultValue("true") boolean enabled,
                             @DefaultValue("5m") Duration ttl,
                             @DefaultValue("10000") @Min(1) long maxEntries) {}
}
```

## 3. `platform-core`: optional per-table TTL

Only the additions to `TableDefinition`:

```java
public final class TableDefinition {
    private final Duration countCacheTtl;            // null => use starrocks.count-cache.ttl

    private TableDefinition(Builder b) {
        // ... existing ...
        this.countCacheTtl = b.countCacheTtl;
    }

    public Optional<Duration> countCacheTtl() { return Optional.ofNullable(countCacheTtl); }

    public static final class Builder {
        private Duration countCacheTtl;
        public Builder countCacheTtl(Duration d) { this.countCacheTtl = d; return this; }
        // ... existing ...
    }
}
```

## 4. `platform-starrocks`: the cache

```java
// platform-starrocks/src/main/java/com/acme/platform/starrocks/cache/CountKey.java
package com.acme.platform.starrocks.cache;

import java.util.Map;

/**
 * Identity of a count = the final COUNT(*) SQL plus its *coerced* bind values.
 * Using the built query (not the raw request) means "2025-01-01T00:00:00" as a String
 * and as a LocalDateTime hit the same entry, and sort/pagination never pollute the key.
 */
public record CountKey(String sql, Map<String, Object> params) {}
```

```java
// platform-starrocks/src/main/java/com/acme/platform/starrocks/cache/CountCache.java
package com.acme.platform.starrocks.cache;

import com.acme.platform.query.TableDefinition;

import java.util.function.LongSupplier;

/** Swap the implementation (e.g. Redis for a shared cross-pod cache) by defining your own bean. */
public interface CountCache {

    /** Returns the cached count, or runs {@code loader} exactly once per key and caches its result. */
    long getOrLoad(TableDefinition def, CountKey key, LongSupplier loader);

    void evict(String table);

    void evictAll();

    static CountCache noop() {
        return new CountCache() {
            @Override public long getOrLoad(TableDefinition d, CountKey k, LongSupplier l) { return l.getAsLong(); }
            @Override public void evict(String table) {}
            @Override public void evictAll() {}
        };
    }
}
```

```java
// platform-starrocks/src/main/java/com/acme/platform/starrocks/cache/CaffeineCountCache.java
package com.acme.platform.starrocks.cache;

import com.acme.platform.query.TableDefinition;
import com.acme.platform.starrocks.StarRocksProperties;
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Cache;
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Caffeine;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Tags;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.binder.cache.CaffeineCacheMetrics;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.function.LongSupplier;

/**
 * One Caffeine cache per table so each table can have its own TTL and its own metrics
 * (cache.gets{result=hit|miss}, cache.size, cache.evictions – tagged with table=...).
 * Caffeine's get(key, mappingFunction) is atomic per key: concurrent requests for the same
 * filters block on one COUNT(*) instead of all hitting StarRocks (no stampede).
 */
public final class CaffeineCountCache implements CountCache {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(CaffeineCountCache.class);

    private final StarRocksProperties.CountCache props;
    private final MeterRegistry registry;
    private final Map<String, Cache<CountKey, Long>> caches = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public CaffeineCountCache(StarRocksProperties.CountCache props, MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.props = props;
        this.registry = registry;
    }

    @Override
    public long getOrLoad(TableDefinition def, CountKey key, LongSupplier loader) {
        Cache<CountKey, Long> cache = caches.computeIfAbsent(def.table(), t -> create(def));
        Long v = cache.get(key, k -> loader.getAsLong());
        return v == null ? 0L : v;
    }

    @Override
    public void evict(String table) {
        Cache<CountKey, Long> c = caches.get(table);
        if (c != null) { c.invalidateAll(); log.info("Evicted count cache for {}", table); }
    }

    @Override
    public void evictAll() {
        caches.values().forEach(Cache::invalidateAll);
        log.info("Evicted all count caches");
    }

    private Cache<CountKey, Long> create(TableDefinition def) {
        Duration ttl = def.countCacheTtl().orElse(props.ttl());
        Cache<CountKey, Long> cache = Caffeine.newBuilder()
                .expireAfterWrite(ttl)
                .maximumSize(props.maxEntries())
                .recordStats()
                .build();
        CaffeineCacheMetrics.monitor(registry, cache, "starrocks.count", Tags.of("table", def.table()));
        log.info("Count cache for {}: ttl={}, maxEntries={}", def.table(), ttl, props.maxEntries());
        return cache;
    }
}
```

## 5. Bundle infra dependencies (`StarRocksQuerySupport`)

Repository constructors were already taking six beans; adding a seventh is the moment to stop. This holder is injected once and every repository becomes `super(support, DEF)`. Future platform additions never touch service code again.

```java
// platform-starrocks/src/main/java/com/acme/platform/starrocks/StarRocksQuerySupport.java
package com.acme.platform.starrocks;

import com.acme.platform.starrocks.cache.CountCache;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import org.springframework.jdbc.core.namedparam.NamedParameterJdbcTemplate;

/** Everything a read repository needs from the platform, in one injectable unit. */
public record StarRocksQuerySupport(NamedParameterJdbcTemplate jdbc,
                                    CursorCodec cursorCodec,
                                    ValueCoercer coercer,
                                    CountCache countCache,
                                    MeterRegistry registry,
                                    StarRocksProperties props) {}
```

```java
// StarRocksAutoConfiguration.java – additions
@Bean
@ConditionalOnMissingBean(CountCache.class)
public CountCache countCache(StarRocksProperties p, MeterRegistry registry) {
    return p.countCache().enabled()
            ? new CaffeineCountCache(p.countCache(), registry)
            : CountCache.noop();
}

@Bean
public StarRocksQuerySupport starRocksQuerySupport(NamedParameterJdbcTemplate jdbc, CursorCodec codec,
                                                   ValueCoercer coercer, CountCache countCache,
                                                   MeterRegistry registry, StarRocksProperties props) {
    return new StarRocksQuerySupport(jdbc, codec, coercer, countCache, registry, props);
}
```

## 6. Repository changes

```java
// platform-starrocks/src/main/java/com/acme/platform/starrocks/AbstractStarRocksReadRepository.java
public abstract class AbstractStarRocksReadRepository<T> {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(AbstractStarRocksReadRepository.class);

    protected final NamedParameterJdbcTemplate jdbc;
    protected final TableDefinition def;
    private final SelectQueryBuilder builder;
    private final CursorCodec cursorCodec;
    private final ValueCoercer coercer;
    private final CountCache countCache;
    private final Timer queryTimer;
    private final Timer countTimer;
    private final long slowQueryThresholdMs;

    protected AbstractStarRocksReadRepository(StarRocksQuerySupport s, TableDefinition def) {
        this.jdbc = s.jdbc();
        this.def = def;
        this.cursorCodec = s.cursorCodec();
        this.coercer = s.coercer();
        this.countCache = s.countCache();
        this.builder = new SelectQueryBuilder(def, s.coercer());
        this.slowQueryThresholdMs = s.props().slowQueryThresholdMs();
        this.queryTimer = Timer.builder("starrocks.query").tag("table", def.table())
                .publishPercentiles(0.5, 0.95, 0.99).register(s.registry());
        this.countTimer = Timer.builder("starrocks.count").tag("table", def.table())
                .publishPercentiles(0.5, 0.95, 0.99).register(s.registry());
    }

    // findPage / findKeyset unchanged

    private Page<T> findOffset(QueryRequest req, Pagination.Offset o) {
        SelectQueryBuilder.SqlQuery q = builder.buildOffset(req, selectColumns());
        RowMapper<T> mapper = rowMapper();

        Page<T> page = timed(queryTimer, q, () -> jdbc.query(q.sql(), q.params(), rs -> {
            List<T> items = new ArrayList<>(q.limit());
            boolean hasNext = false;
            int n = 0;
            while (rs.next()) {
                if (n == q.limit()) { hasNext = true; break; }
                items.add(mapper.mapRow(rs, n++));
            }
            return Page.offset(items, q.limit(), hasNext, o.page(), null);
        }));

        if (!o.includeTotal()) return page;
        return new Page<>(page.items(), page.pageSize(), page.hasNext(), null, o.page(), count(req));
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- counting

    /** Cached count for the request's filters (sort/pagination are ignored for the key). */
    public long count(QueryRequest req) {
        SelectQueryBuilder.SqlQuery q = builder.buildCount(req);
        CountKey key = new CountKey(q.sql(), q.params());
        return countCache.getOrLoad(def, key, () -> executeCount(q));
    }

    /** Bypasses the cache – for admin/ops or when a caller explicitly asks for a fresh number. */
    public long countFresh(QueryRequest req) {
        return executeCount(builder.buildCount(req));
    }

    /** Call after a backfill/reload of this table. */
    public void evictCounts() {
        countCache.evict(def.table());
    }

    private long executeCount(SelectQueryBuilder.SqlQuery q) {
        Long c = timed(countTimer, q, () -> jdbc.queryForObject(q.sql(), q.params(), Long.class));
        return c == null ? 0L : c;
    }

    private <R> R timed(Timer timer, SelectQueryBuilder.SqlQuery q, Supplier<R> body) {
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        try {
            return body.get();
        } finally {
            long ms = TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS.toMillis(System.nanoTime() - start);
            timer.record(ms, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
            if (ms >= slowQueryThresholdMs)
                log.warn("Slow StarRocks query ({} ms) on {}: {} params={}", ms, def.table(), q.sql(), q.params());
            else if (log.isDebugEnabled())
                log.debug("StarRocks query ({} ms): {} params={}", ms, q.sql(), q.params());
        }
    }
}
```

Note that a failed `COUNT(*)` (timeout, FE down) throws out of `getOrLoad` and nothing is cached — Caffeine only stores on successful completion, so a transient failure can't poison the cache.

## 7. Operational eviction endpoint (`platform-web`)

After a nightly reload you'll want to drop stale totals without a restart. This is an Actuator endpoint, so it lives on the management port and under Actuator security — never on the public API.

```java
// platform-web/src/main/java/com/acme/platform/web/CountCacheEndpoint.java
package com.acme.platform.web;

import com.acme.platform.starrocks.cache.CountCache;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.endpoint.annotation.DeleteOperation;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.endpoint.annotation.Endpoint;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.endpoint.annotation.Selector;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

import java.util.Map;

/**
 *  DELETE /actuator/countcache            -> evict all tables
 *  DELETE /actuator/countcache/dw.fact_orders -> evict one table
 */
@Component
@Endpoint(id = "countcache")
public class CountCacheEndpoint {

    private final CountCache cache;

    public CountCacheEndpoint(CountCache cache) { this.cache = cache; }

    @DeleteOperation
    public Map<String, String> evictAll() {
        cache.evictAll();
        return Map.of("status", "evicted", "scope", "all");
    }

    @DeleteOperation
    public Map<String, String> evict(@Selector String table) {
        cache.evict(table);
        return Map.of("status", "evicted", "scope", table);
    }
}
```

```yaml
management:
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: health,info,metrics,prometheus,countcache
```

## 8. Service side — what changes

```java
// OrderTable.java – only if you want to differ from the global 5 minutes
static final TableDefinition DEF = TableDefinition.builder("dw.fact_orders")
        // ... columns, sort, pagination as before
        .countCacheTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(2))   // orders land continuously; keep totals fresher
        .build();
```

```java
// OrderRepository.java – constructor shrinks to this
public OrderRepository(StarRocksQuerySupport support) {
    super(support, OrderTable.DEF);
}
```

Nothing changes in `OrderQueryService` or `OrderController`: `includeTotal=true` now transparently returns a cached total.

If you also want a dedicated count endpoint (so the UI can request the total once and then page with `includeTotal=false`):

```java
// OrderQueryService.java
public long count(OrderSearch s) {
    return repo.count(new QueryRequest(filtersOf(s), List.of(), new Pagination.Offset(1, 0, false)));
}

// OrderController.java
@GetMapping("/count")
public Map<String, Long> count(/* same from/to/customerId/status params */) {
    return Map.of("total", service.count(new OrderQueryService.OrderSearch(from, to, customerId, status,
            List.of(), 0, 1, false)));
}
```

(Extract the filter-building into a private `filtersOf(OrderSearch)` so `search()` and `count()` share it — that keeps the cache key identical for both paths.)

## 9. Things to be aware of

1. **Total and items can disagree slightly.** `hasNext` comes from the live `LIMIT n+1` row; `total` may be up to one TTL old. For an append-only fact table that means the last page might show `hasNext: true` while `page*size >= total`. UIs should trust `hasNext` for navigation and treat `total` as informative — document this in the API.
2. **Key cardinality.** Each distinct filter combination is an entry. Free-form filters (`customerId`, arbitrary time ranges) can produce many keys; `max-entries` bounds memory (an entry is ~ the SQL string + a few boxed values, so 10 000 entries is a couple of MB). Watch `cache_gets_total{result="miss"}` vs `hit` in Prometheus — if the hit rate is low, caching isn't buying you anything for that service and you can set `countCacheTtl` to a longer value or round time ranges to the minute in the controller so near-identical requests share entries.
3. **Multiple instances.** Caffeine is per JVM; with 4 pods you'll run up to 4 counts per key per TTL. That's usually fine. If it's not, implement `CountCache` over Redis (`SETNX` for the lock, `SET … EX ttl` for the value) and declare it as a bean — the `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` makes the platform back off automatically.
4. **Don't cache deep-page `hasNext`.** It's tempting, but it's cheap already (the +1 row) and caching it would make pagination lie.